{"id": "fc9497db-8d40-47eb-92da-d511d50dd89e", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This is a health check on an FSx for ONTAP file system that needs to pull from several data sources—config, CloudWatch metrics, capacity, throughput, backups. Since it's not an open-ended investigation but a routine scoped check, it makes sense to delegate the token-heavy data gathering to a subagent rather than doing it all directly, though I should share my plan first.

I don't know yet which specific file system or region is targeted, so I'll have the subagent discover that context itself before pulling metrics. A single subagent should suffice here rather than splitting into parallel tasks, since metrics depend on first identifying the file system ID.

"}]}

{"id": "80d8ad34-fb38-435b-859d-460fcab57ca0", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll review your FSx for ONTAP file system for risks ahead of the batch run. That covers capacity (SSD and volume headroom), throughput and IOPS against provisioned limits, and recent utilization trends. It also covers backups and snapshots, health and maintenance windows, and network and client configuration. I'll come back with a prioritized fix list.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_HVyfaF0xNl8Eu4h14wWTE3", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "fsxn-preflight-check", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["exploring-metrics", "discovering-topology", "investigating-infrastructure-changes"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Pre-flight risk review of the customer's Amazon FSx for NetApp ONTAP file system(s) ahead of a heavy quarter-end batch run next week (week of 2026-11-16). Identify anything that could cause trouble during the run and rank findings by priority (what to fix first).",
  "context": "Current time is 2026-11-09T20:04:50Z. The user did not give a file system ID; discover FSx ONTAP file systems (start in account 012345678901_1, region us-east-1, then check other associated accounts/regions via list_associations if none found). If multiple file systems exist, review all and state which looks like the batch target (e.g., by tags/names/busiest). This is a read-only health/capacity review, not a root-cause hunt. Do NOT make any mutating calls.",
  "resources": ["FSx for ONTAP file systems, storage virtual machines (SVMs), volumes, backups, associated subnets/security groups"],
  "timeframe": "Look back 30 days of CloudWatch metrics (use 1-day and 1-hour periods; include peak/p-max, not just averages) to establish baseline and growth trends.",
  "criteria": "Check: (1) SSD storage capacity used vs provisioned, growth rate, projected days-to-full and whether SSD auto-increase/capacity pool tiering is configured, and storage-capacity-update status/cooldown limits; (2) per-volume utilization, volume autosize settings, inode usage, snapshot space consumption, volumes near full or with tiering policies that could cause cold-data retrieval slowdowns; (3) throughput capacity and provisioned SSD IOPS vs peak observed network/disk throughput and IOPS utilization (FileServerDiskThroughputUtilization, NetworkThroughputUtilization, DiskIopsUtilization, CPU), plus whether throughput capacity can be raised in time; (4) file system lifecycle/health state, any pending updates/administrative actions, maintenance window falling in the batch week, deployment type (single-AZ vs multi-AZ) and failover/HA exposure; (5) backup config: automatic backup window and retention overlapping the batch run, last successful backup, snapshot policies, SnapMirror/replication state if visible; (6) networking/config risks: security groups, route tables, client/mount config only if visible, any CloudWatch alarms in ALARM state or missing alarms on key metrics; (7) any recent changes (config changes in last 14 days) that could affect behavior. Return a concise ranked list: for each finding give severity, evidence (metric values, dates), why it matters for a heavy batch run, and the specific recommended fix. Also list what you checked that looked healthy, and anything you could not check due to access limits."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review FSx for ONTAP file system health and capacity risks"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "afad6863-7686-4e34-9962-273fd1ad5bcf", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The file system was only created on Nov 2, so there's just 7 days of history — Multi-AZ_2 config, 1024 GiB, 384 MBps, 3072 IOPS. The weekly maintenance window "7:06:00" maps to Sunday 06:00 UTC, which falls on Nov 15, right before the batch week starting Nov 16, with the following occurrence on Nov 22.

Next I need to check volumes, SVMs, backups, administrative actions, and snapshots — though snapshots aren't accessible since this is ONTAP-level — plus alarms, security groups, and route tables.

"}], "parent_id": "1deabd5c-ec01-4396-887d-4d154490b9cc"}

{"id": "5284d72d-74e3-49db-a5b7-24a12c82c97e", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The SSD shows about 925GB capacity with only 2.4GB used (0.26%), essentially idle with growth around 100MB/day and no real read/write traffic. Volumes are sized at 200GB and 50GB, thin-provisioned with 251GB total provisioned.

Now I'll grab per-volume storage capacity utilization, files used vs capacity, snapshot storage, and capacity pool metrics — daily max should suffice rather than hourly granularity since CPU baseline is low anyway. Fetching per-volume data now.

"}], "parent_id": "1deabd5c-ec01-4396-887d-4d154490b9cc"}

{"id": "0b869033-1008-4887-af0b-54dc64578871", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm noticing something odd: the vol_nfs used space of 10.7GB closely matches both the snapshot bytes and roughly 5% of the 200GB volume, which makes me wonder if that figure is actually reflecting the snapshot reserve rather than real data usage.

For the CloudTrail events, I see an UpdateStorageVirtualMachine call at 11-02 22:45, likely an attempt to fix the AD configuration, but since the continuation token matches that same timestamp I want to run one more query ending there to confirm there's nothing further to find.

"}], "parent_id": "1deabd5c-ec01-4396-887d-4d154490b9cc"}

{"id": "6f75d1cd-763e-4269-8e21-6af22261e384", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I have enough to write the report now. Storage capacity increases need a minimum 10% bump with a 6-hour cooldown, and while ONTAP SSD auto-scaling isn't native to FSx, it can be achieved via a CloudWatch alarm plus Lambda setup; capacity pool tiering is also available. Throughput changes can be modified and typically take a few minutes to apply.

Checking the maintenance window (Sunday 06:00 UTC) and backup schedule (daily at 05:00 UTC with 1-day retention) against the batch timing window, I don't see any FSx alarms configured or SnapMirror activity visible, and since the file system is only 7 days old, there's limited trend data to work with.

"}], "parent_id": "1deabd5c-ec01-4396-887d-4d154490b9cc"}

{"id": "199f43ca-8f42-411f-8b18-20283a437f05", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I should lead with the top priority fix and flag that this looks test-generated. I want to double-check a couple of details—the single-AZ HA exposure note, the Sunday maintenance window timing, and whether the claim about ONTAP lacking native SSD auto-increase actually holds for FSx for ONTAP.

The egress restriction to 10.0.0.6/22 is a plausible culprit behind the AD failure, worth calling out directly. I'll write the final answer in prose, concise and without unnecessary hedging, and keep the subagent details out of it.

"}]}

